# The hero's 4K source, 48 MB. Every other build input in image/ is 1-3 MB and
# is tracked; this one is not worth a permanent 48 MB in the history, and git
# keeps a blob forever once it lands. What the site serves is the encode —
# public/media/hero-video.{mp4,webm} — and that is committed. Keep the source
# wherever masters are kept; the ffmpeg command that turns it into those two
# files is in the README.
image/video-upscale.mp4

# The phone hero's source, 19 MB, and out for the same reason as the one above:
# what the site serves is the encode — public/media/hero-video-mobile.{mp4,webm}
# — and that is committed. The ffmpeg command that produces it is in the README.
image/0811.mp4

# The hero's 4K desktop master, 90 MB, and out for the same reason as the two
# above: what the site serves is the encode — public/media/hero-video.{mp4,webm}
# — and that is committed. The phone cut's source is 4.5 MB and is tracked, so
# only this one needs keeping wherever masters are kept.
image/Bg-hero-dekstop.mp4

# The case-study source footage, 135 MB across eight files. What ships is the
# encodes in public/media/na-*.{mp4,webm} — 22 MB, and committed. Same rule the
# two hero masters above are under: the encode is the deliverable, the master is
# not worth a permanent place in the history. The ffmpeg command that produces
# them is in the README.
image/na-video/

# The Overclock introduction film's master, 40 MB, out for the same reason as
# the hero's: what the site serves is the encode —
# public/media/overclock-intro-film.{mp4,webm} — and that is committed. The
# ffmpeg command that produces it is in the README.
image/case-study/overclock/intro-right.mp4
